"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} and §{#} (the solar photosphere, sunspots and the solar cycle). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> four modes, chosen above the picture. Spots: A sunspot, {#} K; A warmer spot, {#} K; A darker spot, {#} K; The spot alone in the sky; No field: no spot. Rotation: Equator against {#}°; One year of winding; If the Sun were solid. Cycle: Cycle {#}, the biggest; Cycle {#}; Now, cycle {#}; The 1870s. Space weather: four CMEs. This worksheet uses <strong>Cycle</strong> and <strong>Spots</strong>.",
"Cycle shows the butterfly diagram (where on the Sun spots appeared, {#} to {#}) above the monthly sunspot number since {#}, with its smoothed curve. Each minimum of the smoothed curve is marked. Move the date by dragging in the picture or with the <strong>Date</strong> slider; with the slider’s knob clicked, the left and right arrow keys move one month at a time. Click the Date readout to type a year, such as {#}.",
"Spots shows a patch of the surface with a sunspot. The sliders set the umbra’s temperature and size and its magnetic field; the check box <strong>Cut the spot out and hang it in the night sky</strong> shows how bright the spot alone would look from Earth.",
