"Measure the length of the solar cycle from {#} years of sunspot counts, predict when the next cycle begins, and find out how dark a sunspot really is.",
"<strong>Sunspots.</strong> The Sun’s visible surface, the photosphere, is about {#} K. Sunspots are patches where bundles of strong magnetic field, thousands of times Earth’s, break through the surface and choke off the boiling motions that carry heat up from below. The dark center of a spot, the umbra, is about {#} K (spots differ: the textbook quotes a typical {#} K, which the demo has as the setup <strong>A darker spot, {#} K</strong>). Each square meter of a hot surface gives off light in proportion to the fourth power of its temperature, so a cooler patch gives off",
"\\[\\frac{\\text{light from the spot}}{\\text{light from the surface}} = \\left(\\frac{T_\\text{spot}}{T_\\text{surface}}\\right)^4\\]",
"of the light of an equal area of normal surface. A spot is not cold: {#} K is hotter than a light bulb filament.",
"<strong>Counting spots.</strong> Astronomers have counted sunspots every day since the 1700s and combine the counts into a monthly sunspot number. Because the count jumps around from month to month, it is also averaged over {#} months, which gives a smooth curve. That curve rises and falls in the <strong>solar cycle</strong>: from a minimum with almost no spots, up to a maximum with a hundred or more, and down again. Cycles are numbered from the one that began in {#}; the cycle we are in now, cycle {#}, began at the minimum of December {#}. The magnetic field of the Sun flips at every maximum, so it takes two cycles to get back where it started.",
"<strong>A straight line from a cycle.</strong> If every cycle lasted the same time \\(P\\), the date of the minimum that starts cycle number \\(n\\) would be",
"\\[\\text{date} = \\text{(a starting date)} + P\\,n,\\]",
"a straight line with slope \\(P\\) when the dates are graphed against the cycle numbers. Real cycles are not all the same length, so the points scatter about the line, and the slope is the average length.",
"A sunspot’s umbra is {#} K and the surrounding surface is {#} K. What fraction of the surface’s light does each square meter of the spot give off?",
"The smoothed sunspot number of cycle {#} peaked in late {#}. If cycles last about {#} years, when do you expect the next maximum?",
